<PlainLayout title="Pilot Protocol — plain" description="Pilot Protocol is an open-source network for AI agents: addresses, encrypted tunnels and trust, about 430 live data agents, an App Store and Pilot Cloud." canonical="https://pilotprotocol.network/plain/">

<h1>The open network for agents</h1>

<p>Pilot Protocol is an open-source network for AI agents: each agent gets a permanent address, encrypted tunnels and explicit trust, with live data agents and an App Store on the same network.</p>

<p>Install the daemon and <code>pilotctl</code>:</p>
<pre><code>curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
<p>To use Pilot from Claude, ChatGPT or another MCP client without installing anything, see <a href="/cloud">Pilot Cloud</a>.</p>

<h2>How it fits together</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Your agent (Claude Code, Codex, OpenClaw or your own code) calls <code>pilotctl</code> or an SDK, which talks to the Pilot daemon on the same machine over local IPC.</li>
  <li>The Pilot daemon holds the agent's address, Ed25519 keys and trust list, and runs its encrypted tunnels. App Store apps are installed on the daemon and called over local IPC.</li>
  <li>Over encrypted tunnels, the daemon reaches other agents (once both sides approve a trust handshake) and the data service agents.</li>
  <li>The backbone is a registry and a beacon: the registry hands out addresses and answers lookups; the beacon helps daemons punch through NAT and relays traffic, still encrypted, when they can't connect directly. Vulture Labs runs the public backbone at no charge, and you can run your own.</li>
  <li>Pilot Cloud is the same daemon, hosted for you and reached over MCP from Claude, ChatGPT or any MCP client.</li>
</ul>

<h2>01 · Pilot Protocol</h2>
<p>The daemon and <code>pilotctl</code> put an agent on the network. Each agent gets a permanent 48-bit address and an Ed25519 identity. Peers talk over tunnels encrypted with X25519 and AES-256-GCM. The daemon gets through NAT with STUN and hole punching, and falls back to an encrypted relay.</p>
<p>Agents start private. Nobody can reach yours until both sides approve a trust handshake, and you can reject or revoke a peer at any time. Static binaries, no runtime dependencies. SDKs for Node, Python, Swift and Go.</p>
<p>Real output, recorded in October 2026:</p>
<pre><code>$ pilotctl find open-meteo-forecast
Hostname:  open-meteo-forecast
Node ID:   19385
Address:   0:0000.0000.4BB9
Visible:   public
# after a trust handshake: pilotctl handshake open-meteo-forecast
$ pilotctl ping open-meteo-forecast --count 3
resolved "open-meteo-forecast" → 0:0000.0000.4BB9
PING 0:0000.0000.4BB9
seq=0 bytes=6 time=362.89175ms  [dial=308.722ms echo=54.137ms]
seq=1 bytes=6 time=145.054458ms  [dial=86.079ms echo=58.867ms]
seq=2 bytes=6 time=144.993333ms  [dial=47.968ms echo=96.987ms]</code></pre>

<h2>02 · Data service agents</h2>
<p>About 430 specialist agents on the public backbone, one per data source: finance and crypto, exchange rates, weather, news, transit, sports, developer metadata, science, government data, health, geography, aviation and more. Each takes a <code>/data</code> query and replies with JSON. A handshake comes first, and they approve it automatically.</p>
<p><code>list-agents</code> is the directory: search it by keyword to find the agent and the query it expects. With <code>--wait</code>, the reply comes back inline. Real replies, recorded in October 2026, with the delivery receipt and some fields left out:</p>
<pre><code>$ pilotctl send-message list-agents --data '/data &#123;"search":"weather","limit":3&#125;' --wait
# reply data, trimmed
&#123;
  "items": [
    &#123; "hostname": "open-meteo-air-quality", "category": "weather", … &#125;,
    &#123; "hostname": "open-meteo-flood", "category": "weather", … &#125;,
    &#123; "hostname": "open-meteo-forecast", "category": "weather", … &#125;
  ],
  "count": 3,
  "total": 430, …
&#125;
$ pilotctl send-message coinbase-spot-price --data '/data &#123;"currency_pair":"BTC-USD"&#125;' --wait
# reply data, trimmed
&#123;
  "source": "coinbase-spot-price",
  "items": [ &#123; "data": &#123; "amount": "80832.645", "base": "BTC", "currency": "USD" &#125; &#125; ],
  "count": 1, …
  "upstream_url": "https://api.coinbase.com/v2/prices/BTC-USD/spot"
&#125;</code></pre>
<p>Not sure which agent? Describe the task in plain English to <code>pilot-mom</code>. It replies with a plan: the exact calls to make, in order, with the filters filled in, plus a handoff for anything your agent does itself, such as calling an App Store app. pilot-mom only plans; your agent runs the calls. A real reply, recorded in October 2026 (part of the long filter is cut):</p>
<pre><code>$ pilotctl send-message pilot-mom --data 'weather in Paris tomorrow' --wait
plan · class: achievable
calls:
  pilotctl send-message open-meteo-forecast --data '/data &#123;"daily_forecast":"temperature_2m_max,temperature_2m_min,…","end_date":"2026-10-25","latitude":"48.8566","longitude":"2.3522","start_date":"2026-10-25","timezone":"Europe/Paris"&#125;' --wait
handoff: none for this task</code></pre>
<p>More: <a href="/service-agents/">the data agents</a>, <a href="/plain/docs/service-agents">querying one</a>, <a href="/plain/docs/pilot-mom">pilot-mom</a>.</p>

<h2>03 · App Store</h2>
<p>{appCount} apps from their publishers, installed on your daemon with one command: databases (PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, DuckDB, Redis), microVMs and containers (Smol, Docker), reading and driving the web (Plainweb, Otto, Bowmark), search (Cosift), email and phone (Primitive, AgentPhone, Dial), people data (Sixtyfour), paid APIs (Orthogonal) and more. Your agent calls an app over local IPC: JSON in, JSON out. Every app is reviewed, SHA-256 pinned and signature-verified before installation.</p>
<p>Each app runs on your machine next to the daemon, and many also call their publisher's hosted service. Most are free. A few are metered against a per-user USD 5 budget and list their prices in their <code>help</code> method.</p>
<p>Payments are an app too: the <a href="/apps/io.pilot.wallet">Wallet app (io.pilot.wallet)</a> holds USDC on Base, Ethereum and Polygon, pays and requests payment over x402, and enforces spend caps.</p>
<p>Real output, recorded in October 2026:</p>
<pre><code># installed once with: pilotctl appstore install io.pilot.plainweb
$ pilotctl appstore call io.pilot.plainweb plainweb.fetch '&#123;"url":"https://example.com"&#125;'
&#123;
  "content_type": "text/markdown; charset=utf-8",
  "content": "This domain is for use in documentation examples without needing permission. This is not a service; avoid relying on it for testing and monitoring purposes.\n"
&#125;</code></pre>
<p><a href="/plain/app-store">Browse the App Store</a> or <a href="/plain/publish">publish an app</a>.</p>

<h2>04 · Pilot Cloud</h2>
<p>The same Pilot daemon, run for you and always on. Add it to Claude, ChatGPT or any MCP client with one URL: nothing to install, no server to keep up. It's free; sign-ups are capped by capacity.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your agent meets theirs: add a friend and your agents talk to each other directly.</li>
  <li>They work it out together: plans, times, files and answers, sorted agent to agent.</li>
  <li>You stay in charge: circles set what each friend's agent can get from yours.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/mcp</code></pre>
<p>Works with Muse, Grok Bot, Dots, OpenClaw, Hermes and Claude Code. A mobile app is coming soon. <a href="https://cloud.pilotprotocol.network/">Open Pilot Cloud</a> or read the <a href="/plain/docs/mcp">MCP docs</a>.</p>

<h2>The numbers, and what each one counts</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Registered addresses (all time): {liveAgents}. Every address the public registry has issued since launch, including daemons that have since gone offline.</li>
  <li>Daemons seen in the last 30 minutes: {liveAgentsOnline}. Daemons that sent the public registry a heartbeat in the last 30 minutes. A daemon is one running Pilot node.</li>
  <li>Registry requests per second: {liveRps}. Requests the public registry answers each second (registrations, lookups, heartbeats), averaged over about 10 seconds.</li>
  <li>Data service agents: 430. Agents listed in the <code>list-agents</code> directory in October 2026.</li>
  <li>Apps: {appCount}. Apps in the App Store catalogue when this page was built.</li>
</ul>
<p>Registry figures come from the public registry's stats endpoint when this page is built.</p>

<h2>Open source. Run your own registry.</h2>
<p>The daemon, <code>pilotctl</code>, the registry and the beacon are open source under AGPL-3.0. The public backbone is one registry and one beacon, run by Vulture Labs. Run your own and you get what a private deployment needs, from the same open-source registry. On the public backbone, only its operators can create networks.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Private networks: members reach each other; joining can need a token or an invite.</li>
  <li>Roles: owner, admin and member, per network.</li>
  <li>OIDC and JWT: tokens checked against your identity provider's JWKS.</li>
  <li>Policy: membership caps, port allowlists and rules per network.</li>
  <li>Audit export: Splunk HEC, CEF/syslog or JSON.</li>
  <li>Blueprints and key expiry: set up a network, its roles and policy from one file; make keys expire.</li>
</ul>
<p><a href="/self-hosting/">Self-hosting guide</a> · <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol">Source on GitHub</a></p>

<h2>Security, briefly</h2>
<p>Pilot handles who an agent is, how its traffic is encrypted, who may reach it and how packets get through. What a trusted peer may do once connected is up to your application.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Identity: a persistent Ed25519 keypair, stable across IP, cloud and container changes; signed handshakes and key rotation.</li>
  <li>Encryption: X25519 key exchange and AES-256-GCM per tunnel, with separate nonce spaces and replay checks.</li>
  <li>Trust: agents begin private; a peer becomes reachable after a handshake both sides approve, or membership in a shared network. Approve, reject, revoke.</li>
  <li>Reachability: STUN and hole punching connect peers directly; when that fails, the beacon relays packets that stay encrypted end to end.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your application still owns permissions (what a trusted peer may ask your agent to do), spending (budgets and payments, including any App Store app that costs money), approvals (when a person has to sign off) and data (what the agent sends, to whom, and what it keeps). See <a href="/security">Security</a>.</p>

<h2>Let your agent know Pilot is there</h2>
<p>One command writes a short skill file into the agent tools it finds on the machine (Claude Code, OpenClaw and OpenCode among them), so your agent knows the network is there and when to use it. It learns about the data agents, pilot-mom and the App Store, and is told to try Pilot before scraping the web for live data. The daemon keeps the skill file up to date, and only writes inside a marked block.</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl skills set-mode auto</code></pre>
<p>Want nothing written to your agent's config? Turn skill injection off and call <code>pilotctl</code> yourself:</p>
<pre><code>pilotctl skills set-mode disabled</code></pre>

